# Findings in git history which gitleaks is right to report and which we
# have accepted. Each line is a fingerprint of the form
#
#     commit:path:rule-id:line
#
# and covers exactly one occurrence in exactly one commit. That
# narrowness is the point: an entry here cannot forgive the same secret
# appearing in a new commit, so re-adding one of these files fails the
# scan again.
#
# Every entry is a claim that something is safe, and history cannot be
# rewritten to make the claim unnecessary -- this repository is public,
# so a secret committed here has been world-readable since the day it
# landed. Adding an entry therefore means the credential has been dealt
# with where it was trusted, not that it has been tidied away. Write
# down which, and how it was dealt with.
#
# Placeholders and documentation examples do not belong here. They recur
# every time the file around them is edited, which would mean a new
# fingerprint each time; they are allowlisted by content in
# .gitleaks.toml instead. See docs/developer_guide/ci.md.

